; Stage 15 W8 G-4, the `(addr-of g)` decision, pinned.
;
; `(addr-of g)` is NOT a read and is never flagged by the ordering check, even
; when `g`'s `defvar` comes later. A global's address is a link-time constant
; that requires no initialization to have happened — G-1 folds it to a bare
; `@g` — and this compiler's own source depends on the property
; (`(vector-new-in (addr-of g-arena-alloc))`). Reading the *value* is what gets
; a zero; taking the address does not.
;
; The forward `(addr-of g)` in a CONSTANT initializer is already covered by
; examples/g1-const-init.nuc. This fixture covers the RUN-TIME path, where the
; exemption is a deliberate carve-out in the walk rather than a consequence of
; the check not running at all: the initializer is a call (so it is queued for
; `@__nucleus_init` and the ordering check does run), and the forward
; `(addr-of …)` sits inside its argument.
;
; The dereference in `main` is what makes the pointer's correctness observable;
; `run_g4_order` links and runs this shape and asserts the value.
(defn g4-ao-wrap (p:ptr:i32):ptr:i32 (return p))

(defvar g4-ao-p:ptr:i32 (g4-ao-wrap (addr-of g4-ao-target)))
(defvar g4-ao-target:i32 7)

(defn main ():i32 (return (deref g4-ao-p)))
